import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="models">

          <p>
            Sign in to Vector to use free models, or connect a provider account or API key. The free models, their
            data policies and their limits are described below.
          </p>

          <h2 id="byok">Bring your own key</h2>
          <p>
            Connect a provider in Settings, or run <code>vector providers login</code> in the terminal, then
            select a model in the session composer. Model availability, context limits, pricing, and
            rate limits come from the provider. Vector's usage view tracks the information returned by
            those sessions.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            From 1.99.99, Vector no longer loads the former third-party hosted providers, even with an API key.
            Choose another provider before starting a task. Sign in with ChatGPT, turned off in desktop 1.99.104 and 1.99.105, is available again from desktop 1.99.106: connect OpenAI, choose ChatGPT Pro/Plus, and use the GPT models your ChatGPT plan includes, or connect OpenAI with an API key. Native
            Copilot, xAI, GitLab OAuth, Poe and DigitalOcean sign-in flows are paused pending Vector's own registrations;
            API-key methods remain where supported.
            External Claude Code, Codex,
            and Cursor runtimes still use your own installed CLIs and their authentication.
          </p>

          <h2 id="free-models">Free models inside of Vector</h2>
          <p>
            Sign in to Vector and the Free models inside of Vector are there at no cost to you: Vector's shared
            allowance runs them on Vector's own OpenRouter account, through Vector's server, which checks your account
            and its limits. You don't need an OpenRouter account of your own. Every model is listed at a zero price
            with tool-capable endpoints, and a request never switches to a paid model or provider. The section appears
            while Vector's service has the shared allowance switched on and has models to serve.
          </p>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.109 and the next CLI release, Vector offers every free model OpenRouter lists, except
            those whose terms bar this use: NVIDIA's trial endpoints, whose terms allow evaluation only, and Google
            Lyria, which charges per song or clip. Each one carries the data policy Vector recorded for the provider
            serving it, reviewed on 17 October 2026:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>No training:</strong> Cohere's North Mini Code (Cohere, kept up to 30 days), Dots3-Note
              Preview (AtlasCloud, which does not publish how long it keeps prompts) and XingChen's Xing4.0 (Phala).
              Each request asks OpenRouter to deny data collection. Google's Gemma 4 26B and 31B (Google AI Studio,
              kept up to 55 days) are listed only for an OpenRouter account of your own, because Google's terms limit
              users in the EEA, Switzerland and the UK to paid use, and the shared allowance does not check where you
              are.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>May train on your prompts:</strong> Poolside's Laguna S 2.1 and XS 2.1 (free until 31 October
              2026), Liquid's LFM2.5-2.6B, Thinking Machines' Inkling and Inkling Small, and OpenRouter's Free Models
              Router. Their providers may log your prompts, code and outputs and use them to train their models. The
              Free Models Router sends each request to a free model OpenRouter picks at random; Vector asks OpenRouter
              to skip NVIDIA's trial endpoints, but OpenRouter doesn't say whether the router honours that. A free model
              Vector has not yet reviewed carries the same label, and its requests still ask OpenRouter to deny data
              collection.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The model picker, the terminal's model list and <code>vector models</code> show
            “May train on your prompts” on those models. Before your first prompt to one, Vector asks you once,
            and nothing is sent until you accept. That one acceptance covers every model with the label, including
            ones Vector has not reviewed yet, until you withdraw it. A prompt to one of them falls back only to
            another such model from the same provider, never to a different provider. An invited guest in a shared
            workspace is not offered these models, since only the workspace owner can accept the notice. Once a guest
            has worked in a session, nothing from that session goes to them, your own prompts in it included, because
            the guest's messages are part of what each request sends. In the terminal,
            <code>vector models --accept-training</code> accepts
            and <code>vector models --decline-training</code> withdraws; in the desktop app, Withdraw is on the
            Free models inside of Vector card in the Help panel. Vector itself never trains on your prompts.
            Vector never picks one of these models for you ahead of a no-training model. Desktop 1.99.108 and
            CLI 1.99.99 are offered no-training models only.
          </p>
          <p>
            The shared allowance has a per-minute and a daily limit for each account, and everyone on it shares
            OpenRouter's limits for Vector's account. A coding task can use many requests, since each tool step is
            one. At a limit, Vector says when the allowance is expected to reset; to keep working before then, choose
            a model from another provider you've connected, or add your own provider key in Settings → Providers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prompts are processed by OpenRouter and the selected model provider. Vector does not request zero data
            retention for these models: most of their providers keep prompts and outputs under their own policies,
            and OpenRouter records Phala, which serves Xing4.0, as keeping none. The policies rely on the providers'
            published terms and OpenRouter's records, not an independent audit. They do not cover Vector's local
            history, OpenRouter's account logging or other connected services. Review them before sending
            confidential code. Other provider connections have their own terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            OpenRouter is also an ordinary provider in Settings → Providers. If you connect an OpenRouter account of
            your own there, its free models are listed under Free models inside of Vector, marked Your OpenRouter
            account, and run on that account's own limits and privacy settings instead of the shared allowance. A key
            stored only in a repository's <code>provider.openrouter.options.apiKey</code> does not list them. From
            1.99.99 to 1.99.108, connecting OpenRouter was the way to start free; from desktop 1.99.109 it is signing
            in to Vector.
            <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/provider-selection">OpenRouter routing documentation</a>
            describes the data-collection and provider controls.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-models">Models in the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector models</code> lists the supported model catalog. Connect a provider with <code>vector providers login</code> to use its models. Pass a provider id to filter and <code>--verbose</code> for metadata such as per-token cost. The catalog is bundled with the release. From 1.99.99, <code>--refresh</code> only fetches when a mirror is explicitly configured with <code>VECTOR_MODELS_URL</code>, <code>VECTOR_MODELS_PATH</code> selects a local catalog, and <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_MODELS_FETCH=1</code> disables refresh.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector models
vector models anthropic --verbose</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            Signed in with <code>vector login</code>, the shared allowance's models are listed under
            <strong>Free models inside of Vector</strong>, marked Shared Vector allowance. Display names omit
            <code>:free</code>; selectors retain it. Human guidance, including the “May train on your prompts” label
            from the next CLI release, goes to stderr, while stdout keeps plain model selectors.
          </p>
          <p>
            Models that expose reasoning effort take <code>--variant</code>. The level resolves from the flag, then your saved preference, then what the session last used, and your choice is kept per provider and model.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
